</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>The Dreamer framework</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dreamer is an <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MBRL</abbr> algorithm that learns a world model and uses imagined trajectories for policy learning, thereby instantiating the Dyna framework <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sutton, 1991</a>)</cite> in a high-dimensional, pixel-based setting.
Here, we briefly recap the DreamerV3 implementation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
The current observation <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is encoded into a discrete stochastic state <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The sequence model predicts the next hidden state <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, from <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the action leading to the next state <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The dynamics are learned by reconstructing the next input <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{x}_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{x}_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The model is summarized below:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Sequence model:}~h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Sequence model:</mtext><mi>h</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Sequence model:}~h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f_{\phi}(h_{t-1},z_{t-1},a_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f_{\phi}(h_{t-1},z_{t-1},a_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Encoder:}~z_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Encoder:</mtext><mi>z</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Encoder:}~z_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Dynamics predictor:}~\hat{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Dynamics predictor:</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Dynamics predictor:}~\hat{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{z}_{t}|h_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>z</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{z}_{t}|h_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Reward predictor:}~\hat{r}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Reward predictor:</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Reward predictor:}~\hat{r}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{r}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{r}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Continuation flag predictor:}~\hat{c}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Continuation flag predictor:</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Continuation flag predictor:}~\hat{c}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{c}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>c</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{c}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Decoder: }~\hat{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Decoder: </mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Decoder: }~\hat{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sim</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{x}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p_{\phi}(\hat{x}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the continuation flag and <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the reward at time <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
The world model is trained with the following loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)=E_{q_{\phi}}\left[\sum_{t}\left(\beta_{\mathrm{recon}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{aux}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{aux}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{rep}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rep}}(\phi)\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>recon</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>recon</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>aux</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>aux</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>dyn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>dyn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)=E_{q_{\phi}}\left[\sum_{t}\left(\beta_{\mathrm{recon}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{aux}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{aux}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{rep}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rep}}(\phi)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">with</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>recon</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\ln p_{\phi}(x_{t}|z_{t},h_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\ln p_{\phi}(x_{t}|z_{t},h_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{aux}}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>aux</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{aux}}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-\ln p_{\phi}(r_{t}|z_{t},h_{t})-\ln p_{\phi}(c_{t}|z_{t},h_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\ln p_{\phi}(r_{t}|z_{t},h_{t})-\ln p_{\phi}(c_{t}|z_{t},h_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>dyn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max(1,\text{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}[\mathrm{SG}(q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t}))||p_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t})])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>D</mtext><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>SG</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max(1,\text{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}[\mathrm{SG}(q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t}))||p_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t})])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rep}}(\phi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rep}}(\phi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\max(1,\text{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}[q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t})||\mathrm{SG}(p_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t}))])\quad," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>max</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext>D</mtext><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>SG</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max(1,\text{D}_{\mathrm{KL}}[q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t})||\mathrm{SG}(p_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t}))])\quad,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where SG is the stop-grad operator and <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{D_{KL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{D_{KL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</abbr>) divergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Reconstruction-free World Model Learning in Dreamer-CDP</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To show how to learn efficient world models without reconstruction, we introduce Dreamer-CDP, a simple variant of DreamerV3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al., 2025</a>)</cite> in which we remove the reconstruction loss while adding a JEPA-style predictor for <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CDP</abbr> (Fig. <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4 Reconstruction-free World Model Learning in Dreamer-CDP ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a,b) inspired by recent work on temporal <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">JEPA</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohammadi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
To that end, we separate Dreamer’s original representation encoder <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:
First, observations <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are mapped to a continuous deterministic embedding <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> via a feature extractor. A stochastic encoder then predicts a latent state representation <math id="S4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(z_{t}|h_{t},u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> from the features <math id="S4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the hidden state <math id="S4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The latent state, together with the current action <math id="S4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, is processed by a recurrent dynamics model to yield <math id="S4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
While a feedforward architecture would be sufficient under full observability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hansen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>,
here we follow the Dreamer lineage in which the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RNN</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RNN</abbr>) is essential to deal with
partial observability.
However, <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RNN</abbr> create distinct challenges for predictive <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SSL</abbr> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mohammadi et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
To navigate these challenges, we train the predictor <math id="S4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}_{t}=g_{\phi}(h_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}_{t}=g_{\phi}(h_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> on the continuous deterministic embeddings <math id="S4.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, which only depend on future observations, not the hidden state <math id="S4.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In contrast to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guo et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we do not use an  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">EMA</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">EMA</abbr>) target network.
Instead, we rely on the insight that the sequence model must be close to a fixed point of its dynamics when the parameters of the representation network are updated <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khetarpal et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
To ensure this convergence, we train the sequence model predictors with a higher learning rate (see <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Hyperparameters ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>) using
the following objective:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\phi)=E_{q_{\phi}}\left[\sum_{t}\left(\beta_{\mathrm{CDP}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{aux}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{aux}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{rep}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rep}}(\phi)\right)\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>CDP</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CDP</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>aux</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>aux</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>dyn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>dyn</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>rep</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\phi)=E_{q_{\phi}}\left[\sum_{t}\left(\beta_{\mathrm{CDP}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{aux}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{aux}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{dyn}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn}}(\phi)+\beta_{\mathrm{rep}}\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{rep}}(\phi)\right)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CDP</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by the negative cosine similarity
<math id="S4.p1.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}(\phi)=-\sum_{t}\cos(\mathrm{SG}(u_{t}),\hat{u}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CDP</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.055em">−</mo><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>cos</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>SG</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}(\phi)=-\sum_{t}\cos(\mathrm{SG}(u_{t}),\hat{u}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth noting that the original Dreamer architecture already incorporates internal prediction through its <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</abbr> balancing mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner, 2021</a>)</cite>, which arises from the <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">KL</abbr> regularization term in the  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ELBO</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">ELBO</abbr>) objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al., 2019b</a>)</cite>.
However, this prediction mechanism leverages the purely probabilistic discrete targets for learning (cf. Fig. <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4 Reconstruction-free World Model Learning in Dreamer-CDP ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>b) and by itself does not lead to high-performing world models, as we will see below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.07083v1/RPL2.png" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:494/159;" width="494" height="159" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S4.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">a)</span> Schematic of Dreamer-CDP. The hidden state is passed through a predictor (green) trained to approximate the next continuous representation <math id="S4.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{u}_{t+1}\approx u_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>u</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{u}_{t+1}\approx u_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In Dreamer, the hidden state and the input embedding are used to predict the next input <math id="S4.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> (dashed gray). <span id="S4.F1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">b)</span> Graphical model of Dreamer (left) and Dreamer-CDP (right) with losses in red. <span id="S4.F1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">c)</span> Visual examples when <math id="S4.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>recon</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Dreamer), <math id="S4.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CDP</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}</annotation></semantics></math> (Dreamer-CDP) or neither is applied. For the latter two, the decoder was trained independently with detached gradients for visualization purposes.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate Dreamer-CDP, we used Crafter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner, 2021</a>)</cite>, a computationally lightweight version of Minecraft, allowing us to assess agents on long-term reasoning, exploration, generalization, and dealing with sparse rewards.
Performance was measured using the Crafter score, a metric that weighs the discovery of new achievements more strongly than the exploitation of already unlocked ones. For instance, unlocking a new achievement that is reached in only 1% of episodes yields a larger score increase than improving the success rate of an existing achievement from 90% to 100%.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation.</span>
We trained all models on a single Nvidia V100 GPU. The model comprised a <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">MLP</abbr> deterministic predictor and a  <span title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RSSM</span> (<abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">RSSM</abbr>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al., 2019b</a>)</cite> with a <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CNN</abbr> encoder (see <a href="#A1.SS1" title="A.1 Hyperparameters ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a> for details). Each model interacted 1M times with the environment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Baseline methods.</span>

We compared Dreamer-CDP to three different algorithms (Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 2 Prior Work ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). First, the original DreamerV3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hafner et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which learns the world model by reconstruction in the input space (Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3 The Dreamer framework ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). We also compared it to MuDreamer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burchi and Timofte, 2024</a>)</cite>, which, inspired by MuZero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schrittwieser et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, trains the world model by predicting the action <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{a}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{a}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t},x_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>a</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{a}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{a}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t},x_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> that lead to the current state and the value <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{v}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{v}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{v}_{t}\sim p_{\phi}(\hat{v}_{t}|h_{t},z_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. MuDreamer attained performance comparable to Dreamer on the Atari and DeepMind Control benchmark, even outperforming it when trained with naturalistic backgrounds. Finally, we also compared it to DreamerPro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, another non-contrastive <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">SSL</abbr> method, which combines prototypical representations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Caron et al., 2020</a>)</cite> with learning a sequence model, but by predicting jointly the cluster assignment of the observation and an augmented view rather than predicting the next latent state.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Dreamer-CDP achieved a Crafter score of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16.2\pm 2.1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>16.2</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>2.1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16.2\pm 2.1\%</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>; Fig. <a href="#A1.F1" title="Figure A.1 ‣ A.2 Supplementary Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.1</span></a>) on par with DreamerV3 (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14.5\pm 1.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>14.5</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14.5\pm 1.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and only outperformed by introducing prioritized experience replay (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.4\pm 1.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>19.4</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.4\pm 1.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kauvar et al., 2023</a></cite>).
To check that these results did indeed depend on the prediction of deterministic target embeddings,
we trained the same model without <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CDP</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CDP}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to classical Dreamer without <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>recon</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{recon}}</annotation></semantics></math>. This manipulation resulted in an expected performance drop (<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.2\pm 1.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.2</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>1.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.2\pm 1.2\%</annotation></semantics></math>; Fig. <a href="#A1.F2" title="Figure A.2 ‣ A.2 Supplementary Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>).
We next compared Dreamer-CDP to other approaches.
MuDreamer exhibited a notably lower Crafter score of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.3\pm 2.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.3</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>2.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.3\pm 2.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> (Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
This performance gap can likely be attributed to the comparatively weak action signal in Crafter.
While we did not train DreamerPro, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kauvar et al. (2023)</a></cite> reported a Crafter score of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.7\pm 0.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.7</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.7\pm 0.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, Dreamer-CDP performs on par with Dreamer and better than previous reconstruction-free approaches.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To check to what extent reward prediction contributed to Dreamer-CDP’s performance, we retrained the model without propagating gradients from the reward predictor head.
We found that this ablation resulted in an intermediate performance drop to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12.7\pm 1.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12.7</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>1.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12.7\pm 1.6\%</annotation></semantics></math> (Fig. <a href="#A1.F2" title="Figure A.2 ‣ A.2 Supplementary Figures ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ Dreamer-CDP: Improving Reconstruction-free World Models Via Continuous Deterministic Representation Prediction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>).
In contrast, when training Dreamer-CDP without the alignment objectives <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn/rep}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mi>dyn</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>rep</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{dyn/rep}}</annotation></semantics></math>, performance decreased to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.3\pm 1.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.3</mn><mo>±</mo><mrow><mn>1.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.3\pm 1.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus <abbr title="" class="ltx_glossaryref ltx_missing">CDP</abbr> is necessary but not sufficient to improve reconstruction-free world models.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Metrics</th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Dreamer</th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">DreamerPro</th>
<th id="S5.T2.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">MuDreamer</th>
